Let $d \geq1$ and $q \geq 2d$ two integers. Consider the four disjoint sets of vertices $U = \{ u_1,u_2,\dots,u_{6d} \}$, $A = \{ a_1,a_2,\dots,a_q \}$, $B = \{ b_1,b_2,\dots,b_q \}$ and $C = \{ c_1,c_2,\dots,c_q \}$. We define the hypergraph $\mathcal{H}_{d,q}$ such that $V(\mathcal{H}_{d,q}) = U \cup A \cup B \cup C$ and $\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{H}_{d,q}) = \{ U \cup A, U \cup B, U \cup C \}$ as illustrated in Figure~\ref{t}. Clearly, $\mathcal{H}_{d,q}$ is $(6d +q)$-uniform, and hence $\chi_p \leq 6d+q$. We next show that $\mathcal{H}_{d,q}$ does not satisfy the conjecture: 
    $$\chi_p(\mathcal{H}_{d,q}) = 6d+q \ , \
    \alpha(\mathcal{H}_{d,q}) = 6d \ , \  \mbox{ and } \ 
    \chi_p^2(\mathcal{H}_{d,q}) = 4d+q > \chi_p - \left\lceil \frac{\alpha}{2} \right\rceil = 3d+q .
    $$
\begin{sloppypar}
We first construct a panchromatic $(6d+q)$-coloring of $\mathcal{H}_{d,q}$. Let $P =\{p_1,p_2,\dots,p_{6d+q} \}$ be a set of colors. For each $i\in \{1,\ldots,6d \}$, assign color $p_i$ to $u_i$. Moreover, for each $j\in \{1,\ldots,q\}$, assign color $p_{6d+j}$ to each of the three vertices $a_j$, $b_j$, and $c_j$. Each hyperedge $U\cup A$, $U\cup B$, and $U\cup C$ therefore contains every color in $P$. Hence, $\chi_p(\mathcal{H}_{d,q})\geq 6d+q$. Together with the bound $\chi_p(\mathcal{H}_{d,q})\leq 6d+q$, this gives $\chi_p(\mathcal{H}_{d,q})=6d+q$.
\end{sloppypar}
Now consider any panchromatic $\chi_p(\mathcal{H}_{d,q})$-coloring. Since every hyperedge contains exactly $\chi_p(\mathcal{H}_{d,q})$ vertices, all vertices within each hyperedge must have distinct colors. In particular, the vertices of $U$, which belong to all three hyperedges, must receive pairwise distinct colors. Furthermore, no vertex in $A\cup B\cup C$ can receive a color assigned to a vertex of $U$, since it will duplicate that color in one of the hyperedges and force another color to be absent. Therefore, each color assigned to a vertex of $U$ appears exactly once in the entire hypergraph. Thus, $\alpha=6d$.

Next, we show that $\chi_p^2(\mathcal{H}_{d,q})=4d+q$. We first construct a bipanchromatic $4d+q$-coloring of $\mathcal{H}_{d,q}$. Let $U$ be partitioned equally into three sets $U_A$, $U_B$, and $U_C$, each of cardinality $2d$. Let $P'= \{p'_1,p'_2,\dots,p'_{4d+q} \}$ be a set of colors. Pair each vertex of $U_A$ with a distinct vertex of $A$, each vertex of $U_B$ with a distinct vertex of $B$, and each vertex of $U_C$ with a distinct vertex of $C$; then, assign a distinct color to the two vertices of each pair. This gives pairs colored with $6d$ distinct colors. Next, $q-2d$ vertices remain uncolored in each of $A$, $B$, and $C$. Group the remaining vertices of these three sets into $q-2d$ triples, each consisting of one vertex from $A$, one from $B$, and one from $C$, and assign a distinct new color to all three vertices of each triple. Therefore, the number of colors used is $6d + (q-2d) = 4d+q$.

\begin{figure}[ht]
    \begin{center}
    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale = 0.7]
        \node[shape=circle] (1) at (1,0) {$u_1$};
        \node[shape= circle] (40) at (1,-0.8) {$\vdots$};         
        \node[shape=circle] (7) at (1,-2) {$u_{6d}$};

        \node[shape=circle,red] (8) at (0,-4.5) {$a_1$};
        \node[shape=circle,red] (100) at (1,-4.5) {$\cdots$};
        \node[shape=circle,red] (11) at (2,-4.5) {$a_q$};

        \node[shape=circle,green] (12) at (-2.2,0) {$b_1$};
        \node[shape=circle,green] (130) at (-2.2,-0.8) {$\vdots$};
        \node[shape=circle,green] (14) at (-2.2,-2) {$b_q$};

        \node[shape=circle,blue] (15) at (4.2,0) {$c_1$};
        \node[shape=circle,blue] (160) at (4.2,-0.8) {$\vdots$};
