\end{figure}

Moreover, this coloring is also an optimal bipanchromatic coloring. Consider an arbitrary bipanchromatic coloring $\phi$ of $\mathcal{H}_{d,q}$, and partition its color classes into two sets $X\cup Y$, where $X$ denotes the set of color classes containing at least one vertex of $U$, and $Y$ denotes the set of color classes containing no vertex of $U$. Since $|U|=6d$, we have $|X| \leq 6d$.
Moreover, every color class in $Y$ must intersect each of the three hyperedges. And since it contains no vertex of $U$, it must contain at least one vertex from each of $A$, $B$, and $C$. Hence, $|Y| \leq q$. In order to maximize the total number of colors, each color class in $X$ should contain exactly one vertex of $U$ and one vertex of $A \cup B \cup C$. Therefore, the classes in $X$ use $6d$ vertices of $A\cup B\cup C$. The remaining $3q-6d$ vertices can then form $\frac{3q-6d}{3}$ color classes in $Y$. Therefore, $|\phi| = 6d+\frac{3q-6}{3} = 4d+q$. Consequently, $\chi_p^2(\mathcal{H}_{d,q}) = 4d+q$.

Let $\varphi$ be a bipanchromatic $k$-coloring  of $H_S(G)$ with color classes $D_1,\dots,D_k$. Consider the set of color classes of total colors denoted by $D' = \{ D_1',\dots, D_m' \} \subset D $. According to the definition of total colors, the colors of $D'$ are mutually pairwise separated; moreover, each color of $D'$ is pairwise separated with all non-total colors. Therefore, according to Theorem~\ref{thm01}, $G$ admits $m$ CIST.

Furthermore, consider regrouping the classes of non-total colors into pairs, so that if only one color remains, it is arbitrarily assigned to another class. These $\lfloor\frac{k-m}{2} \rfloor$ new classes of colors are pairwise separated. Indeed, without loss of generality, consider four arbitrary non-total color classes $ D_1,D_2,D_3,D_4$ (or five classes $D_1$, $D_2$, $D_3$, $D_4$, and $D_5$, when only five classes remain, in which case $D_5$ is merged with the class $D_1$ (\textit{i.e.}, $D_1 := D_1 \cup D_5$)); those classes are regrouped into two classes $A = \{ D_1,D_2 \}$ and $B = \{ D_3,D_4 \}$. According to Lemma~\ref{lemme0}, $A$ and $B$ are connected dominating sets. Let 
\[
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
    F_{AB}^A &= \{ (x,y) \in E(G) | (x \in D_1 \wedge y \in D_4) \wedge (x \in D_2 \wedge y\in D_3) \}\\
    F_{AB}^B &= \{ (x,y) \in E(G) | (x \in D_1 \wedge y \in D_3) \wedge (x \in D_2 \wedge y\in D_4) \}
\end{aligned}
\right.
\]
Those two sets are clearly edge-disjoint and pairwise separated with all total colors; moreover, every vertex of $A$ is incident with an edge of $F_{AB}^A$, and every vertex of $B$ is incident with an edge of $F_{AB}^B$. It follows that $G$ admits $m + \lfloor \frac{k-m}{2} \rfloor = \lfloor \frac{k+m}{2} \rfloor$ CIST.
